  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nSurface high pressure builds from south-central Canada into the \nGreat Lakes region and extending into the upper Ohio Valley regions. \nThis will result in northeast surface winds which will locally \nushering in lower dewpoints and keeping the forecast dry and \npleasant through the remainder of the holiday weekend. A good deal \nof sunshine is forecast both Sunday and Labor Day, with temperatures \nhovering around seasonable levels (upper 70s to around 80).\n\n500mb heights rise after Monday as a northeast mid-level trough \nopens and departs, allowing a central CONUS ridge to shift east \nacross the Ohio Valley. This will result in a surface southwesterly \nflow which will lead into a warming trend for Tuesday and Wednesday, \nwith temperatures climbing back above seasonal levels (mid/upper \n80s). The upper ridge shifts over the coastal Mid-Atlantic and \nNortheast regions in response to a deepening trough over the upper \nGreat Lakes and southern Canada. This will allow a cold front to \npush into the region and serve as a focus for our next round of \nshowers and thunderstorms Wednesday night into Thursday.",
